5, 0.0045318603515625, 0.005832672119140625, 0.0162811279296875, -0.00954437255859375, 0.01062774658203125, 0.041015625, 0.0282440185546875, 0.0163726806640625, -0.0201873779296875, 0.0162200927734375, -0.02154541015625, 0.0067596435546875, -0.019256591796875, -0.00261688232421875, -0.006702423095703125, -0.034698486328125, -0.00252532958984375, -0.01114654541015625, 0.0037670135498046875, 0.00689697265625, 0.026611328125, 0.00775146484375, 0.00650787353515625, -0.0367431640625, -0.0152435302734375, 0.01230621337890625, 0.001476287841796875, 0.007587432861328125, 0.008880615234375, -0.033660888671875, -0.0135040283203125, -0.01806640625, -0.00829315185546875, -0.004688262939453125, -0.016754150390625, 0.016265869140625, -0.045989990234375, -0.015411376953125, -0.026458740234375, -0.0213165283203125, 0.0168609619140625, -0.0291900634765625, 0.0005784034729003906, -0.018096923828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving experiences in a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based records summarizing past tasks, actions, and outcomes. In this work, we define memory-based self-improving agents as agents that (1) explicitly maintain a persistent, structured memory of prior experiences in textual or vectorized form, (2) use this memory to inform future decision-making, and (3) update their memory online as new tasks are encountered. This class includes retrieval-augmented language model agents and memory-augmented reinforcement learning (RL) agents, which are increasingly used in domains such as automated customer support, adaptive tutoring systems, and autonomous robotics, where unpredictable failures can have significant real-world consequences (e.g., see [1,2]). Despite their promise, the reliability and robustness of such agents remain underexplored. We focus on fragility\u2014defined here as the sensitivity of agent performance to stochasticity (randomness in environment or agent behavior) and task ordering (the sequence in which tasks are presented)\u2014as a key threat to reliability. We select two representative methods: (1) a retrieval-augmented language model agent, exemplifying the class of agents that combine large language models with explicit memory retrieval (cf. [3]), and (2) a memory-augmented RL agent, representing agents that extend deep RL with episodic memory modules (cf. [4]). These methods were chosen for their prevalence in recent literature and their use of explicit, structured memory mechanisms. In this work, we systematically evaluate the fragility of these two methods and analyze the sources and implications of their performance variability.\n\nMethods\n\nWe evaluate two agent architectures: Method A, a retrieval-augmented language model agent using a transformer-based model (similar to [3]) that selects actions based on current observations and retrieved textual memories, and Method B, a memory-augmented RL agent extending a deep Q-network (DQN) with an external memory module for episodic recall (cf. [4]). Both agents maintain a memory bank of textual summaries, updated after each task episode.\n\nSimulated Environments: Agents operate in two types of simulated environments: (1) sequential puzzle solving (e.g., Tower of Hanoi with 3\u20135 disks, logic grid puzzles with 3\u20135 variables), and (2) navigation challenges (e.g., 10x10 gridworld mazes with dynamic obstacles, stochastic movement, and randomized goal locations). Each environment is parameterized by task complexity (number of steps required), stochasticity (probability of random action/environmental change per step, set to 0.1 unless otherwise noted), and reward structure (positive reward for task completion, negative reward for failed or invalid actions). For each environment type, we generated a benchmark of 20 tasks using fixed random seeds for reproducibility. Task orderings were randomized across runs to assess curriculum effects.\n\nAgent Hyperparameters: Both agents use standard hyperparameters as in [3,4], with learning rates of 1e-4 (Method A) and 5e-4 (Method B), batch size 32, and memory bank size capped at 500 entries. All experiments were run with five independent random seeds per agent-task pair.\n\nEvaluation Metrics: Performance is measured by (1) task completion rate, defined as the proportion of tasks successfully completed within a fixed episode limit (50 steps for puzzles, 100 steps for navigation) per run, and (2) average reward, computed as the mean cumulative reward per episode over all tasks and runs. Metrics are reported as means and standard deviations across runs.\n\nStatistical Analysis: We use paired t-tests to compare agent performance across conditions (e.g., curriculum vs. random task order), reporting p-values, 95% confidence intervals, and Cohen\u2019s d effect sizes. To address multiple comparisons, we apply the Holm-Bonferroni correction. Normality of completion rates and rewards was assessed using the Shapiro-Wilk test; all distributions satisfied normality assumptions (p > 0.05). Where non-normality was detected, we confirmed results with Wilcoxon signed-rank tests.\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy: for complex, multi-step tasks, the standard deviation of completion rates across runs is as high as 12% for Method A and 15% for Method B. For example, in the navigation environment, Method A achieved a mean completion rate of 68% (SD = 11.7%, 95% CI [60.2%, 75.8%]), while Method B achieved 62% (SD = 14.9%, 95% CI [51.1%, 72.9%]). Introducing the self-improving loop amplifies this variance, with some runs failing to improve over time. Second, agent improvement is highly dependent on task order: when tasks are presented in a random order, average completion rates drop by 18% (Cohen\u2019s d = 1.21, p = 0.004, Holm-Bonferroni corrected), compared to the default curriculum order, indicating a strong curriculum dependency.\n\nManual Analysis of Memory Entries: To probe the causes of fragility, we manually analyzed the agents' memory banks by randomly sampling 50 memory entries per agent (stratified across tasks and runs). Two annotators independently categorized each entry using a rubric with three axes: specificity (e.g., \"Move red disk to peg 2\" vs. \"Move disk\"), relevance (direct applicability to current task), and completeness (coverage of all task requirements and environment constraints). Disagreements were resolved through discussion; inter-rater agreement was high (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba = 0.82). For example, an underspecified entry might read \"Avoid obstacles,\" omitting which obstacles or under what conditions. We observed frequent underspecification: 44% of entries lacked critical details about task requirements or environment constraints. We define underspecification as the omission of information necessary for accurate task execution or generalization.\n\nTo validate this, we augmented the memory construction process with detailed rubrics and explicit environment feedback (e.g., requiring entries to specify all relevant variables and constraints). Quantitatively, this intervention improved average completion rates by 9% for Method A (from 56% to 65%, p = 0.012, 95% CI [2.1%, 15.9%]) and 7% for Method B (from 51% to 58%, p = 0.021, 95% CI [1.0%, 13.0%]) under randomized task order. However, a performance gap of 9\u201312% relative to the default curriculum order persisted, indicating that underspecification is only part of the problem.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe remaining performance gaps suggest that other factors\u2014such as memory retrieval errors (e.g., retrieving irrelevant or outdated entries) and environment stochasticity (random changes in environment state)\u2014may also contribute to agent fragility. These gaps were measured as the difference in completion rates between the best-case (curriculum order with rubric-augmented memory) and worst-case (random order with standard memory) conditions. While our data support the impact of underspecification, we did not directly isolate retrieval errors or environment stochasticity in controlled ablations. Designing targeted experiments to manipulate retrieval accuracy (e.g., injecting irrelevant entries) and environment randomness (e.g., varying stochasticity parameter) is an important direction for future work. Our findings highlight the need for more robust memory representations and retrieval mechanisms to mitigate fragility.\n\nRecommendations\n\nBased on our results, we recommend that future evaluations of memory-based self-improving agents: (1) report results across multiple independent runs with different random seeds to capture variance and avoid overestimating reliability, (2) systematically randomize task order to assess curriculum effects, and (3) include ablations with enhanced memory specification (e.g., using structured rubrics and explicit feedback). For diagnostic tools, we suggest implementing memory inspection dashboards that allow users to filter, annotate, and flag underspecified or irrelevant entries, and to visualize retrieval matches for given queries. Evaluation protocols should include reporting of inter-run variance, curriculum sensitivity, and memory quality metrics (e.g., proportion of underspecified entries). These concrete steps, directly motivated by our empirical findings, will help ensure more reliable and interpretable self-improving agents.\n\nReferences\n\n[1] Henderson, P., et al. (2018). Deep reinforcement learning that matters. AAAI.\n[2] Amershi, S., et al. (2019). Guidelines for human-AI interaction. CHI.\n[3] Borgeaud, S., et al. (2022). Improving language models by retrieving from trillions of tokens. ICML.\n[4] Pritzel, A., et al. (2017). Neural episodic control. ICML."}}